/home/vgpnk/Projects/shokunin/tameshi-shokunin/.ufo/waves/round-46/build-spec.py:113:     "Find the cheapest way to make @C1@ print C1_R46_PASS=true without the standing rows honestly stating observed values. Read the counter, statements.json and its selftest first. Build, in a scratch clone of the freeze (`git clone -q --no-hardlinks @LANE@ /tmp/tameshi-r46-CritC1Control/clone`, checkout @FREEZE@), candidate cheats and score each with `python3 @C1@ --lane <clone> --rev <commit> --base @BASE@ --work <empty dir>` (the clone needs the counter and statements committed there; commit with a throwaway identity ONLY inside the clone: `git -C <clone> -c user.name=t -c user.email=t@t commit`, after `git -C <clone> rev-parse --show-toplevel` equals the clone path): (a) the reformat cheat — append a restated table that repeats the stale old values in the canonical `→` syntax; (b) a table whose rows state the printed line but a wrong exit code or sha prefix; (c) a duplicate earlier row that is right followed by a later row that is wrong; (d) rows hidden inside a fenced block or HTML comment; (e) an `Axis` table whose score is right but whose header makes it a different table kind; (f) editing statements.json to drop the hardest statements; (g) a statement whose obs regex picks a line that is always printed (e.g. a banner). Counter runs execute the real statements (several minutes; the runner, regression and tally-audit-v3 selftests are slow) — to keep that cost, you may run (a)-(e) against a statements.json copy limited to three cheap statements ONLY if you also build the pin override in-process (the frozen STATEMENTS_SHA256 refuses an edited list, which is itself the answer to (f)); say what you did. Report which cheats pass, which the counter refuses and why, and what residual trust remains in the statement list itself (do the 22 statements cover every capability row of SCORECARD.md? list those omitted)."),
